{"authored" [{"question" "e-7352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The number of heads obtained when a coin is flipped $6$ times\n\n- The number of times a $6$-sided die is rolled until a $6$ appears\n\n- The number of months in a particular year" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/example-7352.json" "file_sha256" "dd97f64821c45d87474556d8ae3188a82e6975aea0b5f8f4b6564e666c11df1b" "basis" 2328 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: $x_1, x_2, x_3, \\ldots$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values can be listed in a sequence: $0,1,2,3,4,5,6.$\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can count its possible values in a sequence: $1,2,3,\\ldots$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a discrete random variable. The number of months in a particular year is not the outcome of a trial or experiment. (By definition, there are $12$ months in a year.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/example-7352.json" "file_sha256" "dd97f64821c45d87474556d8ae3188a82e6975aea0b5f8f4b6564e666c11df1b" "basis" 2328 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041842/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-89206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The exact time (not rounded to integers) that a full charge of a phone battery lasts\n- The number of pets owned by a randomly selected family in a town\n- The height of a randomly selected plant in a nursery" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-89206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does not describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,….$\n\n- Statement III does not describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-89206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d5f090659d57266620c302f15e80ff8f2170f3ba96961ee273cc77ce4d521ef6"} {"question" "q-89206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-89206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-89206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The number of seats in a randomly selected sports stadium\n- The volume of milk produced by a cow on a particular day\n- The distance that a particular sprinter can run in $1$ minute" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, we cannot list the possible real-numbered values in a sequence.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d5f090659d57266620c302f15e80ff8f2170f3ba96961ee273cc77ce4d521ef6"} {"question" "q-83762" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83762" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83762" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}, & x=3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}, & x=7\\end{aligned}\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}, & x=3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}, & x=7\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}, & x=3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}, & x=7\\end{aligned}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0\\le f(x)\\le 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{x\\in S}f(x)=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability mass function because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of $f$ do not sum to $1:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{7}{6}\\ne 1\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability mass function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n0\\le \\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}\\le 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=1.\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because the value $h(3)=\\frac{3}{2}$ does not satisfy the first condition (it is not between $0$ and $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83456" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d5f090659d57266620c302f15e80ff8f2170f3ba96961ee273cc77ce4d521ef6"} {"question" "q-83456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f$ and $g$ only"] ["text" "$f$ only"] ["text" "$f,$ $g,$ and $h$"] ["text" "$g$ and $h$ only"] ["text" "$g$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83456" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$g$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-124711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}, & x=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}, & x=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}, & x=\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{25}, & x=0 \\\\ \\frac{23}{25}, & x=1\\end{aligned}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-124711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0\\le f(x)\\le 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{x\\in S}f(x)=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of $f$ do not sum to $1:$\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{1}{5}\\ne 1\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability mass function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n0\\le \\frac{1}{5},\\frac{4}{5}\\le 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}=1.\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability mass function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n0\\le \\frac{2}{25},\\frac{23}{25}\\le 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{25}+\\frac{23}{25}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-124711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d5f090659d57266620c302f15e80ff8f2170f3ba96961ee273cc77ce4d521ef6"} {"question" "q-124711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-124711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f$ and $g$ only"] ["text" "$f$, $g$ and $h$"] ["text" "$g$ and $h$ only"] ["text" "$g$ only"] ["text" "$h$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-124711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$g$ and $h$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X=x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n\nCompute $P(X\\in {2,6,8})$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\in {2,6,8}) & =P(X=2)+P(X=6)+P(X=8) \\\\  & =0.2+0.2+0.3 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83773" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d5f090659d57266620c302f15e80ff8f2170f3ba96961ee273cc77ce4d521ef6"} {"question" "q-83773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.4"] ["math" "0.5"] ["math" "0.6"] ["math" "0.7"] ["math" "0.8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83773" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "| $x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X=x)$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{4}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n\nCompute $P(X\\in {\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}})$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\in {\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}}) & =P(X=\\frac{1}{2})+P(X=\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{4}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d5f090659d57266620c302f15e80ff8f2170f3ba96961ee273cc77ce4d521ef6"} {"question" "q-83457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-83457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{9}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-323123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "| $y$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n\nCompute $P(-1<Y\\le 1)$ given that the random variable $Y$ has the probability mass function $f(y)$ shown in the table above.\n\n$P(-1<Y\\le 1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-323123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1<Y\\le 1) & =P(Y\\in {0,1}) \\\\  & =P(Y=0)+P(Y=1) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-323123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d5f090659d57266620c302f15e80ff8f2170f3ba96961ee273cc77ce4d521ef6"} {"question" "q-323123" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-323123" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-124767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n\nCompute $P(X\\le 0)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-124767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 0) & =P(X\\in {-2,-1,0}) \\\\  & =P(X=-2)+P(X=-1)+P(X=0) \\\\  & =f(-2)+f(-1)+f(0) \\\\  & =0.1+0.2+0.2 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-124767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d5f090659d57266620c302f15e80ff8f2170f3ba96961ee273cc77ce4d521ef6"} {"question" "q-124767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-124767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.1"] ["math" "0.2"] ["math" "0.3"] ["math" "0.5"] ["math" "0.7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "q-124767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ce82ebaddd151b47dda661a231ba5cede883bf0656473474c7a080678579119"} {"question" "e-7352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The number of heads obtained when a coin is flipped $6$ times\n\n- The number of times a $6$-sided die is rolled until a $6$ appears\n\n- The number of months in a particular year" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/example-7352.json" "file_sha256" "47aefcd5a8d69c26f01cd63c7dcf29ce7804729042c5323524e56a10ac36eca5"} {"question" "e-7352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: $x_1, x_2, x_3, \\ldots$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values can be listed in a sequence: $0,1,2,3,4,5,6.$\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can count its possible values in a sequence: $1,2,3,\\ldots$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a discrete random variable. The number of months in a particular year is not the outcome of a trial or experiment. (By definition, there are $12$ months in a year.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/example-7352.json" "file_sha256" "47aefcd5a8d69c26f01cd63c7dcf29ce7804729042c5323524e56a10ac36eca5"} {"question" "e-7353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/9e8f3f1bb50432add6ba6c1b91fb5e1f7ab052e48ab155c2e92b40bc309e81a5.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/example-7353.json" "file_sha256" "51fb867fd10a7e6bdd883a26c5beb40d291db48744fcea38b5a326c3d9b2bc4d"} {"question" "e-7353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/9363d37d53374519f1170d2c1c24ecb690a6810a304207ef21a0d1f3eba609de.png) to be a valid probability mass function on a set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/5c3d9b70452a08b50ba5b826f5694c3d85c999754e5304ff71d47362a66534cd.png) it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/53ad6d309b94b7889b85ecc5d90ae0201d2ba7b25b2b82cc9b6c95d1165ba268.png) for all ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/86d18b1a03e111c64a7328566ad27a5ae12870cf2ee67e9750f5747bee71eb86.png) in ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/d4aaadce376b13dc707757d84b05abf9c95d436aa2e4bea8fb19a078eff136c6.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/024a7717c9fccd8dceb278a0a50934b514706a425b43f76ee9d229b9008b822b.png)\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/06a568b15033fbfbfc33c59b4f4c1b9382df53efb53025fa9bdf5714972c7440.png) is *not* a valid probability distribution because the value ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/d14540db55ffd098075a55e4c744e9cde80545517df1456959c8baea7500586e.png) does not satisfy the first condition (it is not between ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/637d7cec1da56f96138c07b108310c3d842ddc13b466234f78b4ee1757882a1b.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/5d9e7c41ca12f816fdb84db36a35ba66280982891484cbee9db4a7222c863d92.png)).\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/3d7612998c8458ff973fe19d9fa000175d6ab8ce6d2689d593852f0311e73b6b.png) is *not* a valid probability distribution because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/49f6627729021cf5e3e78f4dcbedb957495a385bccdb4484e5a5ac6f892b0161.png) do not sum to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/19ef2d374cfb64bc575f920e7413112e512fdda510e0fa71dfbd8297cf05d96d.png)\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/cb3ebaf420b7fc49cf896463ad37b95855da99cc6356e4795dc81df2d76f5503.png)\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/70bff1a95629c275ea93a10e6130b14e6565bb70465a778e1bff38526ad389da.png) is a valid probability distribution. Indeed, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/5392675a2894798e20c813a2bc0611940d9f37373f85ed4e474016d673d9a5e3.png)\nand\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/10c830df4267667cf3475f1a41b45d5ec27949c1395b00b7502325481dc93830.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/3d8cbf1521bc8f20e8ad2dff7bf427089ee3e1e591cdba51102049daab5d70f5.png) only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/example-7353.json" "file_sha256" "51fb867fd10a7e6bdd883a26c5beb40d291db48744fcea38b5a326c3d9b2bc4d"} {"question" "e-7355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P\\left(X\\in \\{2,4,6\\}\\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/example-7355.json" "file_sha256" "333ca1300031bb8ee2a626975b045e505b98011c58e2680bae24006927fda492"} {"question" "e-7355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\in {2,4,6}) & =P(X=2)+P(X=4)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(4)+f(6) \\\\  & =0.1+0.1+0.2 \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/example-7355.json" "file_sha256" "333ca1300031bb8ee2a626975b045e505b98011c58e2680bae24006927fda492"} {"question" "e-7354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X=x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ |\n\nCompute $P\\left( X \\leq 3 \\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/example-7354.json" "file_sha256" "76060a8411e961081ba7e7a7bd56b0db888acde9f7d5cd0d0258a0f6f2c88388"} {"question" "e-7354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(x\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/example-7354.json" "file_sha256" "76060a8411e961081ba7e7a7bd56b0db888acde9f7d5cd0d0258a0f6f2c88388"}] "usage" {"e-7353" [] "q-83456" [] "q-83457" [] "e-7355" [] "e-7352" [] "e-7354" []} "basis" 2347 "retractions" [[17592186106428 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{1}{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3},\\end{vmatrix}x = 7g(x) = {\\frac{1}{3},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3},\\end{vmatrix}x = 7h(x) = {\\frac{3}{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2},\\end{vmatrix}x = 7$"] [17592186106428 :question/answer-fields 17592186105077] [17592186106429 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X = x)$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{4}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n\nCompute $P(X ∈ {\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{3}{2}})$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above."] [17592186105576 :question/problem "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{2}{5},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5},\\end{vmatrix}x = 1; \\frac{4}{5}, \\mid x = 2g(x) = {\\frac{2}{5},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{10},\\end{vmatrix}x = 1h(x) = {\\frac{3}{10},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2},\\end{vmatrix}x = 1; \\frac{1}{5}, \\mid x = 2$"] [17592186105576 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function on a set $S$, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because the value $f(1)=-\\dfrac15$ does not satisfy the first condition (it is not between $0$ and $1$).\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of $h$ do not sum to $1{:}$\n$\\dfrac25 + \\dfrac{3}{10} = \\dfrac{7}{10} \\neq 1$\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability distribution. Indeed, we have\n$0 \\leq \\dfrac{3}{10}, \\dfrac12, \\dfrac15 \\leq 1$\nand\n$\\dfrac{3}{10} + \\dfrac12 + \\dfrac15 = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\""] [17592186105578 :question/problem "Compute $P\\left(X\\in \\{2,4,6\\}\\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table above.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |"] [17592186105578 :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X ∈ {2, 4, 6}) &= P(X = 2) + P(X = 4) + P(X = 6) \\\\\n&= f(2) + f(4) + f(6) \\\\\n&= 0.1 + 0.1 + 0.2 \\\\\n&= 0.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105577 :question/problem "Compute $P\\left(X \\leq 3 \\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X=x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ |"] [17592186105577 :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3) &= P(x ∈ {1, 2, 3}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 1) + P(X = 2) + P(X = 3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{3}{10} + \\frac{1}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186105078 17592186105079 17592186105080 17592186105081 17592186105082 17592186105084 17592186105085 17592186105086 17592186105087 17592186105088] "immutable_fields" [[17592186105077 :answer-field/correct] [17592186105077 :answer-field/id] [17592186105077 :answer-field/key] [17592186105077 :answer-field/type] [17592186105083 :answer-field/correct] [17592186105083 :answer-field/id] [17592186105083 :answer-field/key] [17592186105083 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
